{"name":"Easter Green","hex":"#8cfd7e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"easter","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Eastern. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","comparative form of east: more east"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An easterly wind (a wind blowing from the east); especially, a strong one; a storm of such winds."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/easter"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Easter","blocks":[{"text":"Easter, also called Pasch or Pascha or Resurrection Sunday, is a Christian celebration and cultural holiday commemorating the resurrection of Jesus from the dead, described in the Bible's New Testament as having occurred on the third day of his burial following his crucifixion by the Romans at Calvary c. 30 AD. It is the culmination of the Passion of Jesus, preceded by Lent, a 40-day period of fasting, prayer, and penance."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Chora_Anastasis2.jpg/330px-Chora_Anastasis2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Easter","wikidata":"Q21196"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/P%C3%B6tting_Kirchenfenster_7_Osterlamm.jpg/500px-P%C3%B6tting_Kirchenfenster_7_Osterlamm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/P%C3%B6tting_Kirchenfenster_7_Osterlamm.jpg/1280px-P%C3%B6tting_Kirchenfenster_7_Osterlamm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/P%C3%B6tting_Kirchenfenster_7_Osterlamm.jpg/500px-P%C3%B6tting_Kirchenfenster_7_Osterlamm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A stained-glass window depicting the Passover Lamb, a concept integral to the foundation of Easter[39][43]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AP%C3%B6tting_Kirchenfenster_7_Osterlamm.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/The_Last_Supper_-_Leonardo_Da_Vinci_-_High_Resolution_32x16.jpg/500px-The_Last_Supper_-_Leonardo_Da_Vinci_-_High_Resolution_32x16.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/The_Last_Supper_-_Leonardo_Da_Vinci_-_High_Resolution_32x16.jpg/1280px-The_Last_Supper_-_Leonardo_Da_Vinci_-_High_Resolution_32x16.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/The_Last_Supper_-_Leonardo_Da_Vinci_-_High_Resolution_32x16.jpg/500px-The_Last_Supper_-_Leonardo_Da_Vinci_-_High_Resolution_32x16.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Last Supper (1495–1498). Mural, tempera on gesso, pitch and mastic, 700 x 880 cm (22.9 x 28.8 ft). In the Santa Maria delle Grazie Church, Milan, Italy, it is Leonardo da Vinci's dramatic interpretation of Jesus' last meal before death. The Last Supper celebrated by Jesus and his disciples. The early Christians, too, would have celebrated this meal to commemorate Jesus's death and subsequent resurrection.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Last_Supper_-_Leonardo_Da_Vinci_-_High_Resolution_32x16.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Nikea-arius.png/500px-Nikea-arius.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Nikea-arius.png/500px-Nikea-arius.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The First Council of Nicaea, with Arius depicted as defeated by the council, lying under the feet of Emperor Constantine","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANikea-arius.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Calendario_pasquale.jpg/500px-Calendario_pasquale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Calendario_pasquale.jpg/1280px-Calendario_pasquale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wiki